mary": "The lack of fever, left shift, or infection markers increases the likelihood of reactive/non-malignant leukocytosis over infectious or malignant causes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute constitutional symptoms and elevated leukocyte count support chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (CML) over myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts?", "summary": "Subacute symptoms and a high white blood cell count are more consistent with chronic-phase CML than acute myeloid leukemia with increased blasts."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could constitutional symptoms and elevated WBC represent a plasma cell disorder like multiple myeloma?", "summary": "Systemic symptoms and leukocytosis are atypical for plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma, which typically present with anemia, monoclonal protein, and bone pain."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fatigue) argue against reactive leukocytosis?", "summary": "Weight loss and fatigue are less common in reactive leukocytosis and more suggestive of neoplastic processes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal leukocyte differential with elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase argue against myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts (AML)?", "summary": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase is more characteristic of chronic myeloproliferative disorders than AML, which typically shows decreased alkaline phosphatase."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of lymphocytosis or monoclonal plasma cell features argue against lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders?", "summary": "Lack of lymphocytosis or monoclonal features reduces the likelihood of lymphoma or plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.341], ["B2", 0.272], ["B1", 0.258]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B3) posterior (0.34) <0.75; dangerous alternative B2 (danger 0.8, posterior 0.27) remains unresolved; tree depth (2) < max depth (3); available discriminators (e.g., BCR-ABL testing for CML) have not been exhausted"}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.27244582043343657, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Lymphocytosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B3", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.341}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "posterior": 0.272, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase is more characteristic of chronic myeloproliferative disorders than AML, which typically …"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1", "B4", "B3.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=71%, source=both]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic Phase:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Polycythemia Vera (PV):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, burning sensation in extremities\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Essential Thrombocythemia (ET):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic Phase vs Polycythemia Vera (PV):\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, burning sensation in extremities, confusion\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic Phase vs Essential Thrombocythemia (ET):\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic Phase vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF):\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, bleeding easily, blurred vision\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, anorexia\n\nPolycythemia Vera (PV) vs Essential Thrombocythemia (ET):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n\nPolycythemia Vera (PV) vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nEssential Thrombocythemia (ET) vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF):\n  Favours first:  abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, amaurosis fugax, autosomal dominant inheritance\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, anorexia\n\nNOT typically seen in Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF): autoimmune antibody positivity, hemophagocytosis\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic Phase: blast phase chronic myelogenous leukemia, bcr-abl1 positive, familial chronic myelocytic leukemia-like syndrome, leukemia, myeloid, accelerated-phase\n\nRelated to Polycythemia Vera (PV): acquired secondary polycythemia, congenital secondary polycythemia, polycythemia (disease)\n\nRelated to Essential Thrombocythemia (ET): familial thrombocytosis, myeloproliferative neoplasm, thrombocytosis disease\n\nRelated to Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF): acquired aplastic anemia, cellular phase chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis, familial myelofibrosis\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
